          {
            "localId": "ohm_series_integrated",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_1"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1"
            ],
            "title": "Learn and apply Ohm's law to one simple series circuit",
            "activityType": "Integrated concept-and-practice task",
            "purpose": "Teach the needed constructs and immediately apply them to a worked example, then to a changed circuit solved independently, so the accepted outcome is met in one bounded task.",
            "instructions": "Work through this in one sitting. Use only the methods taught here: V = I × R, and for a series circuit the current is the same through every component while the source voltage equals the sum of the resistor voltage drops.\n\n1. Read the constructs. Voltage V (volts) is the electrical push across a component. Current I (amperes) is the rate of charge flow. Resistance R (ohms) opposes current. Ohm's law states V = I × R for a resistor, equivalently I = V / R and R = V / I. In a simple series circuit there is one path, so the same current I flows through every resistor; the source voltage V_total is shared as voltage drops V_1 = I × R_1, V_2 = I × R_2, and V_total = V_1 + V_2.\n\n2. Study this worked example. A 12 V source is connected to R_1 = 2 Ω and R_2 = 4 Ω in series. Total resistance R_total = 2 + 4 = 6 Ω. Current I = V_total / R_total = 12 / 6 = 2 A. Voltage drops: V_1 = 2 A × 2 Ω = 4 V; V_2 = 2 A × 4 Ω = 8 V. Check: 4 V + 8 V = 12 V, matching the source. The current is 2 A through both resistors because the series path is single.\n\n3. Now solve this changed circuit independently, without looking back at the worked example. A 9 V source is connected to R_1 = 3 Ω and R_2 = 6 Ω in series. Compute: (a) total resistance, (b) current through the circuit, (c) voltage drop across each resistor, (d) a check that the voltage drops sum to the source voltage. Write your numbers with units.\n\n4. Explain in your own words: why is the current the same through both resistors, and how do the voltage drops relate to the source voltage? Then state one way you would recognize a series circuit from a diagram.\n\nIf you want extra practice after finishing, you may optionally repeat step 3 with a 24 V source and R_1 = 4 Ω, R_2 = 8 Ω, but this is not required for completion.",
