            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The whole plan addresses this scope: building a method-selection repertoire and applying it to unfamiliar circuits."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "method_repertoire",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "select"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1"
            ],
            "title": "Build a method-selection repertoire for unfamiliar circuits",
            "activityType": "Guided method-selection study",
            "purpose": "Establish the decision rules that connect circuit features to the correct analysis method, with a short embedded diagnostic of current selection habits.",
            "instructions": "Work through a short set of contrasting circuits you write out yourself (or copy from your course notes): (1) a single-source series circuit, (2) a circuit with two independent sources, (3) a circuit with a dependent source, (4) a circuit where one branch current is the only unknown, (5) a circuit with a supernode or supermesh. For each, before calculating anything, write one sentence naming the method you would choose (series/parallel reduction, voltage divider, current divider, nodal analysis, mesh analysis, superposition, source transformation, Thevenin/Norton) and the circuit feature that drives that choice (number of nodes vs meshes, presence of dependent sources, whether one branch quantity is wanted). Then solve each circuit fully and check your answer by an independent route (e.g. nodal then mesh, or KVL around a loop). Keep a one-page decision table: circuit feature -> method -> why. Use only methods you have already been taught; if a circuit needs a method you have not covered, note it as a gap rather than guessing. This first pass also serves as your diagnostic: mark any circuit where your first method choice was wrong.",
            "completionCriterion": "A completed decision table covering at least five contrasting circuit features, with each circuit solved and checked, and any wrong first choices marked.",
