{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "A runnable report.py that reads your own expenses.csv, prints correct per-category totals, and reports each malformed row with its row number and reason."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "ROADMAP",
          "outcome": "Build a small CSV expenses report in Python that groups by category, reports totals, and checks malformed rows. I have never read a CSV in Python.",
          "explanation": "You have never read a CSV in Python, so the useful next checkpoint is one small working report script, not the whole eventual project. The script itself teaches csv.reader, dictionary grouping, and malformed-row checks in one integrated task: you create a tiny expenses CSV, read it, group by category, total amounts, and flag rows that fail a simple contract (wrong number of fields or non-numeric amount). This is enough to demonstrate the accepted outcome at beginner scale. Later engineering (larger files, pandas, richer validation, charts, packaging) stays strategic and unbooked until this checkpoint works and you ask for more. The 90-minute task exceeds the 60-minute daily ceiling, so it is split at a genuine educational boundary: first get reading, grouping, and totals working on valid rows; then add malformed-row detection and reporting. Each piece is a complete, runnable continuation of the same script.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 60,
            "likelyMinutes": 90,
            "highMinutes": 150
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "phase_report",
              "title": "Working CSV expenses report",
              "outcome": "A runnable Python script that reads a small expenses CSV, groups by category, prints totals, and reports malformed rows.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 60,
                "likelyMinutes": 90,
                "highMinutes": 150
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "Script runs on your own sample CSV and produces correct per-category totals plus a clear list of malformed rows."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Taught and used inside the report script: open the file, use csv.reader, skip or flag bad rows."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_2",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Taught and used inside the report script: accumulate totals per category with a dictionary."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_3",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Taught and used inside the report script: detect wrong field count and non-numeric amounts, report them with row numbers."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_4",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The checkpoint deliverable: the small expenses report script itself."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "build_report",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_report"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2",
              "scope_3",
              "scope_4"
            ],
            "title": "Build and run a small CSV expenses report",
            "activityType": "Guided beginner programming task",
            "purpose": "Integrate CSV reading, category grouping, totals, and malformed-row checks into one working script so the accepted outcome is demonstrated end to end.",
            "instructions": "Create a file named expenses.csv in your project folder with a header row and at least six data rows, for example:\ncategory,amount\nfood,12.50\ntransport,3.00\nfood,7.25\nfun,10.00\nfood,notanumber\ntransport,4.50,extra\n\nThen write a Python script report.py that:\n1. Opens expenses.csv and reads it with csv.reader.\n2. Treats the first row as the header and expects exactly two columns: category and amount.\n3. For each remaining row, checks that it has exactly two fields and that the amount can be converted to a number with float(). If either check fails, print a line like: Row 6 malformed: amount 'notanumber' is not a number. Do not add that row to the totals.\n4. For valid rows, add the amount to a running total for that category using a dictionary.\n5. After reading all rows, print each category and its total, for example: food: 19.75.\n6. Run the script and confirm the output matches your hand calculation for the valid rows, and that both malformed rows are reported with their row numbers.\n\nUse only the Python standard library (csv, no pandas). If you get stuck on a specific line, print the row and the error message to see what is happening. Keep the script small and readable.",
            "completionCriterion": "report.py runs without crashing on your expenses.csv, prints correct per-category totals for the valid rows, and reports each malformed row with its row number and a clear reason.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 60,
              "likelyMinutes": 90,
              "highMinutes": 150
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 90,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [],
            "splitPoints": [
              {
                "afterMinutes": 45,
                "completionCriterion": "report.py reads expenses.csv with csv.reader, groups valid rows by category, and prints correct per-category totals for the valid rows."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [
            "You can run Python scripts locally and create a text file.",
            "You are new to csv.reader but can follow concrete step-by-step instructions."
          ],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "The first checkpoint uses only the standard library and a tiny hand-made CSV; pandas, large-file handling, richer validation, and reporting formats are deferred to keep the first working result small and achievable.",
            "The 90-minute task is split at 45 minutes because the 60-minute daily ceiling cannot hold it whole; the split keeps the same script and final criterion, adding malformed-row handling only after totals work."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "Your exact Python setup (version, editor) is unknown; the instructions assume a standard Python 3 environment."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop when report.py produces correct totals and malformed-row reports on your own expenses.csv. Do not expand to larger datasets or new libraries until you have demonstrated this checkpoint and explicitly ask for the next milestone."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "The 90-minute report task cannot fit the 60-minute daily ceiling, so it is split at a genuine educational boundary: first make reading, grouping, and totals work on valid rows; then add malformed-row detection and reporting. This preserves the full task, total effort, and final criterion while fitting the known hard slot. No work was removed or shortened."
}

