{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "You can explain Bayes' rule in your own words, correctly apply it to a changed medical-test example, and state why the base rate and sensitivity play different roles."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "MASTERY",
          "outcome": "Develop the ability to explain Bayes' rule and apply it to a changed medical-test probability example, distinguishing the base rate from sensitivity.",
          "explanation": "The learner's stated confusion is specifically between base rate (prevalence) and sensitivity (true positive rate). The smallest useful path is one integrated activity that builds the natural-frequency representation, derives Bayes' rule from it, and applies it to a changed medical-test example. Because the learner reports no prior completion and no readiness evidence, the activity begins with a brief concrete introduction to the needed constructs rather than assuming them. A second, contingent activity is reserved for the specific case where the first attempt shows the confusion persists; it is not compulsory because the integrated task may resolve the confusion cleanly.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 45,
            "likelyMinutes": 60,
            "highMinutes": 90
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "phase_1",
              "title": "Bayes' rule for medical tests",
              "outcome": "Explain Bayes' rule and apply it correctly to a changed medical-test example, clearly separating base rate from sensitivity.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 45,
                "likelyMinutes": 60,
                "highMinutes": 90
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "You can state the rule, compute the posterior for a new example, and explain in your own words why the base rate and sensitivity are different quantities."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Introduced through natural frequencies in the integrated activity so the learner can see the base rate as the prevalence of disease before any test result."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_2",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Derived from the natural-frequency table and stated as a formula, then applied to the changed example."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_3",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The changed medical-test example is the transfer task; the learner must solve it without a worked solution."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "bayes_integrated",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_1"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2",
              "scope_3"
            ],
            "title": "Explain and apply Bayes' rule to a changed medical-test example",
            "activityType": "Integrated explanation and application",
            "purpose": "Build the natural-frequency representation, derive Bayes' rule, and apply it to a new medical-test example so the learner can distinguish base rate from sensitivity.",
            "instructions": "Work through this in one sitting if you can, or split it across two short sessions. Use only the methods taught here; do not use advanced Bayesian software or methods you have not seen.\n\n1. Start with a concrete population. Imagine 10,000 people. A disease has prevalence (base rate) 1%, so 100 people have it and 9,900 do not. A test has sensitivity 90% (it detects 90% of people who have the disease) and specificity 95% (it correctly clears 95% of people who do not have the disease).\n\n2. Build a natural-frequency table with four counts: diseased and test positive; diseased and test negative; not diseased and test positive; not diseased and test negative. Compute each count from the numbers above.\n\n3. From the table, write the fraction of test-positive people who actually have the disease. This is the posterior probability P(disease | positive test). Compute it as a decimal and a percentage.\n\n4. Now write Bayes' rule in symbols: P(D | +) = P(+ | D) P(D) / [P(+ | D) P(D) + P(+ | not D) P(not D)]. Match each symbol to the numbers you used in the table. Explain in your own words why P(D) is the base rate and P(+ | D) is the sensitivity, and why they are not the same quantity.\n\n5. Changed example: A different disease has prevalence 5%. A new test has sensitivity 80% and specificity 90%. Using the same natural-frequency method (start with 10,000 people), compute the posterior probability that a person who tests positive actually has the disease. Show your table, your fraction, and your final percentage.\n\n6. Check your changed example by substituting the same numbers into the Bayes formula from step 4. Do the two methods agree? If not, find and correct the error.\n\n7. Write a short explanation (3-5 sentences) of what would happen to the posterior if the base rate were much lower while sensitivity stayed the same, and why. Use your own numbers to support the explanation.\n\nIf you get stuck on step 5, go back to the table in step 2 and rebuild it slowly with the new numbers before using the formula.",
            "completionCriterion": "You produce a correct natural-frequency table and posterior for the changed example (prevalence 5%, sensitivity 80%, specificity 90%), the two methods agree, and your written explanation correctly distinguishes base rate from sensitivity and describes the effect of a lower base rate with the same sensitivity.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 45,
              "likelyMinutes": 60,
              "highMinutes": 90
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 60,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": []
          },
          {
            "localId": "bayes_remediation",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_1"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2"
            ],
            "title": "Targeted base-rate vs sensitivity remediation",
            "activityType": "Contingent focused practice",
            "purpose": "Resolve a persisting base-rate/sensitivity confusion only if the integrated activity shows that specific error.",
            "instructions": "This is only needed if your work on the integrated activity shows you are still swapping the base rate and sensitivity. If so, do the following: take the changed example from the integrated activity and write two separate sentences: one that states the base rate in plain language, and one that states the sensitivity in plain language. Then recompute the posterior using only the natural-frequency table, and label each number in the table as either 'base-rate information' or 'test-accuracy information'. Finally, explain in one paragraph why a low base rate can make a positive test result less conclusive even when sensitivity is high.",
            "completionCriterion": "You correctly label the base rate and sensitivity in the changed example, recompute the posterior correctly, and explain the effect of a low base rate without swapping the two quantities.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 20,
              "likelyMinutes": 30,
              "highMinutes": 45
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 30,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "CONTINGENT",
              "trigger": {
                "checkpointActivityId": "bayes_integrated",
                "condition": "Your changed-example posterior is wrong because you used sensitivity where the base rate belongs, or you cannot explain the difference between base rate and sensitivity in your own words."
              }
            },
            "hardDependencies": [
              "bayes_integrated"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [
            "The learner has not yet demonstrated readiness in any of the three scope areas, so the integrated activity begins with a brief concrete introduction to the needed constructs."
          ],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "A single integrated activity is preferred over separate explanation, practice, and check stages because the outcome is one bounded performance goal and the learner's confusion is specific; splitting into ritual stages would add time without adding evidence.",
            "The contingent remediation is not compulsory because the integrated task may resolve the confusion cleanly; forcing it would violate the mastery policy's preference for conditional remediation."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "Whether the learner's confusion will persist after the natural-frequency introduction is unknown; the contingent activity addresses that possibility without assuming it."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop when the learner can correctly apply Bayes' rule to the changed example and explain the base-rate/sensitivity distinction; no further practice is required unless the contingent trigger fires."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "The plan uses one committed integrated activity because the accepted outcome is a single bounded performance goal and the learner's reported confusion is specific. A contingent remediation is included only for the concrete case where the integrated activity shows the base-rate/sensitivity confusion persists. This avoids compulsory preparatory lessons or ritual check stages that would add time without adding evidence, while still providing a targeted response if the integrated task does not resolve the confusion."
}

